// expect: refuse E_FnShadowsOp
// `spawn` is an op of lib/threading's Thread effect, and a program
// links that module through the manifest. Declaring a function by the same
// name was silent: the env is append-only and read last-write-wins, and an
// effect decl re-registers its ops AFTER the entry module's declarations
// (measured order for this name: op, fn, fn, fn, op, op). So the fn loses,
// every call performs the op, and the body is judged against the op's
// signature — the only symptom being `Int vs () -> t...` at the author's
// own call, naming a shape they never wrote.
//
// It lives in tests/frontier rather than tests/micros because the micro
// harness feeds a blob on stdin whose link does NOT include threading —
// the collision is invisible there, which is what sent two earlier probes
// into a program that never had it.
fn spawn(a) = 42

fn main() = spawn(7)
